    "q-334118": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334118",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x+1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{′} & ({x}^{2})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(x+1)\\cdot (2x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x(x+1)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x \\\\  & =x(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-334118-0",
          "tag": "custom-select",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "0",
              "dom_id": null,
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{x}^{2}-1",
              "html": "<mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"66\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-msup><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D465 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.363em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msup><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2212\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"89\" style=\"font-size: 119.6%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-msup><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D465 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.363em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msup><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2212\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>"
            },
            {
              "option": "1",
              "dom_id": null,
