    "q-334257": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334257",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-12y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nIII. The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}})+2x(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})-12(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x)+2x(3{x}^{2})-12({x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
